## Suggestions for improvement (prioritized)

**§A — Close the PASS/FAIL/REVIEW/UNVERIFIED/NOT_APPLICABLE gap deliberately, not mechanically.**
This is the highest-value fix and the one the brief repeats most (sections 4, 5, 6, 8, 11, 12 all depend on it). Recommended shape: keep `NOT_APPLICABLE` meaning "vendor/config genuinely has no such concept" (already correct in `evaluate.rego`), and add a real `UNVERIFIED` branch in `result_for()` that fires when the baseline carries an explicit "this came from a low-confidence/AI-normalized, not-yet-human-approved" marker for that parameter — which requires `SecurityBaselineModel.flatten()` to emit a sibling signal (e.g. a `_meta.unverified_parameters: [dotted_path, ...]` list) rather than overloading `null`. Propagate `UNVERIFIED`/`REVIEW` through `OPADecision`, the `Finding.result` column (still a free-text column, so no migration needed — just a new allowed value), `compute_score()`'s "applicable" exclusion set (alongside `NOT_APPLICABLE`), the dashboard counters, and the frontend `RESULTS` filter + a "Review in Training Center" link using the existing `CommandMapping`/Training Center pages. Do this as one deliberate PR with its own tests, not folded into unrelated work — it touches Rego, two Python services, the DB write path, and two frontend pages.

**§B — Build the Part 2 §14 demo as an actual test, not just documentation.**
Add a Cisco fixture with `ssh version 1`, run it through the real parser → `SecurityBaselineModel` → `opa_service.evaluate_baseline()` (respx-mocked or against a real OPA if CI has one) and assert `CIS-SSH-001` comes back `FAIL`/`HIGH` with the expected evidence line; then do the same for a Juniper fixture expressing the equivalent SSHv1 config and assert it produces the *same* `control_id` and result. This single test is the most direct, checkable proof of "vendor syntax ≠ compliance logic" the brief asks for, and currently doesn't exist as an executable artifact anywhere in the repo.

**§C — Decide whether "custom policy" means (a) wiring `UnifiedControl` rows into OPA's input/bundle at evaluation time, or (b) a narrower "add a row to `controls.py` + `controls.rego`" workflow with the new parity test as its guardrail.** Option (a) is architecturally bigger (needs OPA to either accept ad-hoc rules per tenant via a data document, or a per-tenant bundle) but matches the brief's "without changing vendor parser code" framing more literally, since `UnifiedControl` already exists and is tenant-scoped. Option (b) is much cheaper and the parity test added in this pass makes it safe, but a custom control today still requires a code change + deploy, not a config action a compliance admin could take themselves.

**§D — Fix `compute_score()`'s all-`NOT_APPLICABLE` edge case** (returns 100.0 with zero applicable controls) before it's the actual value on any dashboard — pair with a UI badge ("N/A — no applicable controls evaluated") rather than a bare number, per the brief's explicit warning against misleading scores.

**§E — Persist `policy_version` and OPA's per-finding `reason` string onto the `Finding` row.** Both are already computed in `opa_service`/`evaluate.rego` and simply dropped in `compliance.py::opa_decision_to_findings`. This is the cheapest fix in this document (two extra dict keys + one migration column) and directly closes the finding→policy traceability chain the brief calls out in §6.

**§F — Add a vendor filter to the Compliance page.** Either denormalize `vendor` onto `Finding` at write time (cheapest, small migration) or join through `Scan → Device` server-side; the frontend filter UI pattern already exists for framework/severity and can be copied.

**§G — General hygiene:** the presence of the three now-deleted `package compliance.ignore` stub files suggests there may be other build/flatten artifacts elsewhere in the tree from the same source event; worth a repo-wide `find . -name "*__*.py" -o -name "*__*.rego"` sweep before the next milestone to catch siblings this pass didn't specifically look for.

